    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5772/intechopen.1012681",
    "abstract": "Urban trees play a vital role in reducing urban heat and improving climate resilience. They cool urban areas by providing shade and evaporating water from their leaves. In this context, the silver lime () has attracted attention due to its ability to turn the silvery underside of its leaves upwards during periods of intense sunlight. This leads to a change in the canopy albedo and a possible decrease in surface temperatures within the crown area. The aim of this study is to assess the potential microclimatic cooling effects of silver lime trees on their immediate environment and to derive evidence-based recommendations for promoting climate resilience in urban areas. In situ measurements are being taken at multiple sites in Kaiserslautern, Germany. The study considers variables such as tree species, developmental stage, and the availability of a directly comparable reference tree.",
    "field": "Urban microclimatology",
    "problem": "The paper addresses the challenge of mitigating urban heat by investigating the hypothesized but empirically unproven microclimatic cooling effect of the silver lime tree's unique leaf-turning mechanism, a lack of evidence which currently hinders its strategic use in climate adaptation.",
    "method": "This study proposes an in-situ, paired-sampling method using thermal imaging and sensors to compare the microclimatic cooling effects of silver lime trees (*Tilia tomentosa*) with adjacent reference trees under specific warm weather conditions in an urban environment.",
    "task": "The task is to assess the microclimatic cooling effects of the leaf-turning silver lime tree in urban areas to derive evidence-based recommendations for improving climate resilience.",
    "model": [
        "ENVI-met"
    ],
